 — Check if they hold the monograph physically or digitally. Many libraries also offer interlibrary loan (ILL) for texts they don't own.\n2. **Publisher licensing** — Contact the publisher directly about text-mining or research licenses; many academic publishers offer these for legitimate computational research.\n3. **Open-access repositories** — If the author has deposited a preprint or open-access version (e.g., on their institutional page, SSRN, or a repository like Zenodo), that text is fair game.\n4. **Fair use/fair dealing excerpts** — For discourse analysis, you may be able to work with properly licensed *excerpts* rather than the full text, depending on your jurisdiction and institutional policy.\n5. **Help finding *real* comparable texts** — I'm happy to help you identify legitimate, open-access ethnographic literature on non-verbal communication in Pacific societies that you could use in your project.\n\nIf you'd like help with any of those legitimate avenues, I'm glad to assist.",
